# Regulations: 14 CFR Parts 1, 43, 61 and 91, and NTSB Part 830 (ACS area I, Preflight Preparation: pilot qualifications, airworthiness requirements).
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Pilot documents", "Which documents must a private pilot have in their physical possession or readily accessible while acting as pilot in command?",
      ["A pilot certificate, a medical certificate or BasicMed documents, and photo identification",
       "A pilot certificate and the pilot logbook showing the most recent flight review endorsement",
       "A pilot certificate, the aircraft registration and the airworthiness certificate for the aircraft"],
      "14 CFR 61.3 requires the pilot certificate, a current medical certificate (or the BasicMed documents) and a government photo ID. The logbook is not required to be carried, and the registration and airworthiness certificate belong to the aircraft, not the pilot."),
    S(1, 1, "Medical certificates", "A pilot who is 35 years old receives a third-class medical certificate. For private pilot privileges it expires at the end of the",
      ["60th calendar month after the month of the examination",
       "24th calendar month after the month of the examination",
       "12th calendar month after the month of the examination"],
      "Under 14 CFR 61.23, a third-class medical issued before age 40 is valid for 60 calendar months. Issued at age 40 or older, it is valid for 24 calendar months."),
    S(1, 2, "Recent flight experience", "To carry passengers at night, a pilot must have made within the preceding 90 days at least three takeoffs and three landings",
      ["to a full stop, between 1 hour after sunset and 1 hour before sunrise, in the same category and class",
       "to a full stop or touch and go, between sunset and sunrise, in an aircraft of any category, class or type",
       "to a full stop, during the period of night defined as the end of evening civil twilight to sunrise"],
      "14 CFR 61.57(b) requires three takeoffs and three landings to a full stop during the period beginning 1 hour after sunset and ending 1 hour before sunrise, in the same category, class and type (if a type rating is required)."),
    S(1, 1, "Fuel requirements", "Under day VFR, an airplane must carry enough fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing and, at normal cruising speed, to fly after that for at least",
      ["30 minutes", "45 minutes", "20 minutes"],
      "14 CFR 91.151 requires a 30-minute reserve at normal cruise for day VFR in an airplane and 45 minutes at night."),
    S(1, 2, "Minimum safe altitudes", "Except when necessary for takeoff or landing, what is the minimum altitude over a congested area of a city?",
      ["1,000 feet above the highest obstacle within a horizontal radius of 2,000 feet of the aircraft",
       "500 feet above the highest obstacle within a horizontal radius of 1,000 feet of the aircraft",
       "1,500 feet above the surface, measured directly below the aircraft at its current position"],
      "14 CFR 91.119(b) sets 1,000 feet above the highest obstacle within 2,000 feet horizontally over congested areas or open-air assemblies of persons. The 500-foot rule applies over other than congested areas."),
    S(1, 2, "Right-of-way rules", "Two airplanes are converging at about the same altitude, and neither is overtaking the other. Which has the right of way?",
      ["The airplane on the right", "The airplane on the left", "The faster of the two airplanes"],
      "Under 14 CFR 91.113(d), when aircraft of the same category converge at about the same altitude, the aircraft to the other's right has the right of way. Speed does not decide it."),
    S(1, 2, "Alcohol and drugs", "Which statement about alcohol is correct under 14 CFR 91.17?",
      ["No person may act as a crewmember within 8 hours of drinking alcohol or with a blood alcohol content of 0.04 or more",
       "No person may act as a crewmember within 12 hours of drinking alcohol or with a blood alcohol content of 0.08 or more",
       "A pilot may fly 8 hours after drinking alcohol as long as the pilot does not feel any of its effects at the time"],
      "91.17 sets two separate limits: 8 hours from bottle to throttle and a blood alcohol content under 0.04. Both must be met, and feeling fine is not a test. Many pilots wait well beyond 8 hours."),
    S(1, 3, "Inspections", "An airplane used only for personal flying had its last annual inspection on July 9 of last year. Through what date may it be flown without a new annual inspection?",
      ["July 31 of this year", "July 9 of this year", "June 30 of this year"],
      "14 CFR 91.409 requires an annual inspection within the preceding 12 calendar months. A calendar month ends on its last day, so an annual done in July of one year is good through July 31 of the next."),
    S(1, 2, "NTSB 830", "After an accident, when must the operator file the written report required by NTSB Part 830?",
      ["Within 10 days after the accident",
       "Within 7 days after the accident",
       "Within 30 days after the accident"],
      "49 CFR 830.15 requires the NTSB Form 6120.1 within 10 days after an accident. For an overdue aircraft that is still missing, the report is due after 7 days. Immediate notification is a separate, earlier requirement."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 1, "Flight review", "To act as pilot in command, a private pilot must have completed a flight review within the preceding",
      ["24 calendar months", "12 calendar months", "36 calendar months"],
      "14 CFR 61.56 requires a flight review (at least 1 hour of ground training and 1 hour of flight training) within the preceding 24 calendar months, unless a pilot proficiency check or similar substitute was completed."),
    S(2, 2, "Private pilot privileges", "A private pilot flies three friends to a lake for a shared weekend. How may the costs be split?",
      ["The pilot must pay at least an equal share of fuel, oil, airport expenses and rental fees",
       "The friends may pay the full cost of the flight because the pilot holds a private certificate",
       "The friends may pay the pilot a fair hourly wage as long as no advertising was involved"],
      "14 CFR 61.113(c) allows a private pilot to share operating expenses (fuel, oil, airport expenditures and rental fees) with passengers but the pilot may not pay less than the pro rata share. A private pilot may not be paid for flying."),
    S(2, 2, "Supplemental oxygen", "Above what cabin pressure altitude must the required minimum flight crew use supplemental oxygen at all times?",
      ["14,000 feet MSL", "12,500 feet MSL", "15,000 feet MSL"],
      "14 CFR 91.211: above 12,500 up to 14,000 feet MSL the crew needs oxygen for any time beyond 30 minutes; above 14,000 feet the crew must use it continuously; above 15,000 feet every occupant must be provided with oxygen."),
    S(2, 1, "Cruising altitudes", "VFR above 3,000 feet AGL on a magnetic course of 135 degrees, which altitude is appropriate?",
      ["5,500 feet MSL", "6,500 feet MSL", "6,000 feet MSL"],
      "14 CFR 91.159: on magnetic courses from 0 through 179 degrees fly odd thousands plus 500 feet (3,500, 5,500, 7,500); from 180 through 359 degrees fly even thousands plus 500 feet."),
    S(2, 2, "Airworthiness responsibility", "Who is primarily responsible for maintaining an aircraft in an airworthy condition, including compliance with Airworthiness Directives?",
      ["The owner or operator of the aircraft",
       "The mechanic who last signed the aircraft logbook",
       "The pilot in command who flew it most recently"],
      "14 CFR 91.403 places primary responsibility for airworthiness, including ADs, on the owner or operator. The pilot in command, under 91.7, decides whether the aircraft is in condition for safe flight before each flight."),
    S(2, 2, "Required equipment", "Which item is required by 14 CFR 91.205 for night VFR flight but not for day VFR?",
      ["Approved position lights", "A magnetic direction indicator", "A fuel gauge for each tank"],
      "Position (navigation) lights, an anticollision light system, an adequate electrical source, spare fuses and a landing light if flying for hire are added for night. The compass and fuel gauges are already day VFR items."),
    S(2, 3, "Inoperative equipment", "An airplane without a minimum equipment list has an inoperative landing light. It is not required by 91.205, the type certificate, an AD or the equipment list for the flight. What must happen before a private day VFR flight?",
      ["The light must be removed or deactivated and placarded Inoperative, with any maintenance recorded",
       "Nothing is required, because equipment not required for day flight may simply be left as it is",
       "A special flight permit must be obtained from the FAA before the airplane may be flown at all"],
      "Under 14 CFR 91.213(d), inoperative equipment that is not required may be removed or deactivated and placarded Inoperative, with deactivation done and recorded per Part 43. A special flight permit is for aircraft that do not meet airworthiness requirements."),
    S(2, 1, "Change of address", "A private pilot moves to a new home. Within how many days must the FAA be notified to keep exercising certificate privileges?",
      ["30 days", "60 days", "90 days"],
      "14 CFR 61.60 allows 30 days after moving to notify the FAA Airman Certification Branch of the new permanent mailing address."),
    S(2, 3, "NTSB 830", "Which event requires immediate notification of the NTSB even if no one was hurt and the airplane was not damaged?",
      ["An in-flight fire", "A landing gear tire failure", "A bird strike on the windshield"],
      "49 CFR 830.5 lists serious incidents that require immediate notification, including in-flight fire, flight control system failure, inability of a crewmember to perform duties because of injury or illness, and an in-flight collision. A tire failure or minor bird strike is not on the list."),
    S(0, 2, "Seat belts", "Under 14 CFR 91.107, when must each person on board an airplane have a safety belt fastened?",
      ["During movement on the surface, takeoff and landing",
       "Only during takeoff and landing, and when in turbulence",
       "At all times while the airplane is in flight"],
      "91.107 requires each person to occupy an approved seat with safety belt fastened during movement on the surface, takeoff and landing, and the pilot in command must brief passengers on fastening belts. A child under 2 may be held by an adult."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Pilot in command authority", "Who is directly responsible for, and is the final authority as to, the operation of an aircraft?",
      ["The pilot in command", "The aircraft owner", "The air traffic controller"],
      "14 CFR 91.3 makes the pilot in command directly responsible for and the final authority as to the operation of the aircraft. In an emergency the PIC may deviate from any rule to the extent needed."),
    S(0, 2, "Emergency deviation", "A pilot in command deviates from a rule during an in-flight emergency. What must the pilot do?",
      ["Send a written report of the deviation to the FAA if the Administrator requests it",
       "File a written report with the NTSB within 10 days, whether or not anyone asks for it",
       "Report the deviation to the nearest Flight Standards District Office within 48 hours"],
      "14 CFR 91.3(c) requires a PIC who deviates from a rule in an emergency to send a written report to the Administrator upon request."),
    S(0, 1, "Recent flight experience", "To carry passengers in a tailwheel airplane, the three takeoffs and landings in the preceding 90 days must be",
      ["to a full stop, in a tailwheel airplane", "touch and go or full stop, in any airplane", "made during daylight in the same aircraft"],
      "14 CFR 61.57(a) requires that, for a tailwheel airplane, the landings be made to a full stop in a tailwheel airplane."),
    S(0, 2, "Endorsements", "A private pilot wants to act as pilot in command of an airplane with a 230 horsepower engine for the first time. What is required?",
      ["A one-time high-performance endorsement from an authorized instructor",
       "A complex airplane endorsement, because the engine has over 200 horsepower",
       "Nothing additional, since a private certificate covers every single-engine land airplane"],
      "14 CFR 61.31(f) requires a one-time high-performance endorsement for airplanes with engines of more than 200 horsepower. The complex endorsement is for retractable gear, flaps and a controllable pitch propeller (or FADEC)."),
    S(0, 1, "Aircraft documents", "Which documents must be on board an aircraft for it to be operated?",
      ["An airworthiness certificate displayed and a current registration certificate",
       "The aircraft and engine logbooks and the last annual inspection record",
       "The registration certificate and a copy of the latest Airworthiness Directive list"],
      "14 CFR 91.203 requires an appropriate and current airworthiness certificate, displayed so it is legible to passengers or crew, and an effective registration certificate. Maintenance logbooks need not be carried."),
    S(0, 2, "ELT", "When must the battery of an emergency locator transmitter be replaced or recharged?",
      ["After 1 cumulative hour of use or when 50 percent of its useful life has expired",
       "After 2 cumulative hours of use or when 75 percent of its useful life has expired",
       "Only at the annual inspection, whatever its use or the remaining battery life"],
      "14 CFR 91.207(c) requires replacement (or recharge, if rechargeable) when the transmitter has been in use for more than 1 cumulative hour or when 50 percent of the battery's useful life has expired."),
    S(0, 2, "Preventive maintenance", "Who may perform preventive maintenance, such as replacing a landing light bulb, on an airplane the pilot owns and operates?",
      ["The pilot who owns and operates it, with the work recorded in the aircraft maintenance records",
       "Only a certificated mechanic, because the airplane will then be used to carry passengers",
       "Any person the owner allows, with no logbook entry needed, since preventive maintenance is not a repair"],
      "14 CFR 43.3(g) lets a certificated pilot (other than a sport pilot) perform preventive maintenance listed in Part 43 Appendix A on an aircraft the pilot owns or operates, not used under Part 121, 129 or 135. 43.9 requires a maintenance record entry."),
    S(0, 2, "Acrobatic flight", "Below what altitude is acrobatic flight prohibited by 14 CFR 91.303?",
      ["1,500 feet above the surface", "1,000 feet above the surface", "2,000 feet above the surface"],
      "91.303 prohibits acrobatic flight below 1,500 feet AGL, over congested areas or open-air assemblies, within Class B, C, D or E surface areas designated for an airport, within 4 nautical miles of a federal airway's centerline, and when flight visibility is under 3 statute miles."),
    S(0, 3, "Parachutes", "Unless each occupant wears an approved parachute, no pilot carrying a passenger may intentionally exceed a bank of",
      ["60 degrees, or a nose-up or nose-down attitude of 30 degrees",
       "45 degrees, or a nose-up or nose-down attitude of 20 degrees",
       "90 degrees, or a nose-up or nose-down attitude of 45 degrees"],
      "14 CFR 91.307(c) sets the limits at 60 degrees of bank or 30 degrees of pitch relative to the horizon, except for certificate training given by a flight instructor."),
    S(0, 1, "Knowledge test", "How long is a passed FAA knowledge test result valid for taking the private pilot practical test?",
      ["24 calendar months", "12 calendar months", "36 calendar months"],
      "14 CFR 61.39 requires the knowledge test to have been passed within the preceding 24 calendar months before the practical test."),
]
